    40	## 1. The corrected object and its filtration
    41	
    42	Let \(k\) be any field, \(R=k[[t]]\), \(u=t^6\), and
    43	
    44	\[
    45	 \theta=t\frac d{dt},\qquad \Theta=u\frac d{du}.
    46	\]
    47	
    48	The coefficient field may be \(\mathbf F_p\), a finite extension, or its
    49	algebraic closure. This is a \(t\)-adic lifting theorem over that same field;
    50	it is not a lift from characteristic \(p\) to characteristic zero.
    51	
    52	The six allowed tail families split into the following 30 \(u\)-streams:
    53	
    54	- residues \(0,1,2,3,4,5\) for each of
    55	  `tf1, tf2, tg1, tg2`;
    56	- residues \(0,2,4\) for each of `tg01, tg02`.
    57	
    58	Fix the deterministic column order by family first and then increasing
    59	allowed residue:
    60	
    61	\[
    62	\begin{array}{c|c|c}
    63	q&\text{family}&\text{residues in order}\\ \hline
    64	1\!:\!6&\mathrm{tf1}&0,1,2,3,4,5\\
    65	7\!:\!12&\mathrm{tf2}&0,1,2,3,4,5\\
    66	13\!:\!18&\mathrm{tg1}&0,1,2,3,4,5\\
    67	19\!:\!24&\mathrm{tg2}&0,1,2,3,4,5\\
    68	25\!:\!27&\mathrm{tg01}&0,2,4\\
    69	28\!:\!30&\mathrm{tg02}&0,2,4.
    70	\end{array}                                                \tag{1.0}
    71	\]
    72	
    73	For a stream of residue \(\rho\), its global \(t\)-shift is
    74	
    75	\[
    76	 r_\rho=6+\rho\quad(\rho\ne4),\qquad r_4=16,              \tag{1.1}
    77	\]
    78	
    79	where \(r_4=16\), rather than \(10\), records `PIN42`. Thus
    80	
    81	\[
    82	 X=\bigoplus_{q=1}^{30}t^{r_q}k[[u]],\qquad \sum_qr_q=288. \tag{1.2}
    83	\]
    84	
    85	The corrected target is
    86	
    87	\[
    88	 Y^+=\bigoplus_{a=0}^{29}t^{s_a}k[[u]]e_a,               \tag{1.3}
    89	\]
    90	
    91	where
    92	
    93	\[
    94	 s_a=6+2((a+1)\bmod3)\quad(0\le a\le28),\qquad
    95	 s_{28}=16,\qquad s_{29}=36,                             \tag{1.4}
    96	\]
    97	
    98	and \(\sum_as_a=276\). Every direct sum here is a finite direct sum of
    99	formal-series spaces, hence a product in the coefficient direction.
   100	
   101	Embed these spaces in their global \(t\)-series ambient spaces and put
   102	
   103	\[
   104	 F^nM=M\cap t^nM_{\rm amb}.
   105	\]
   106	
   107	Equivalently, for
   108	\(x=(t^{r_q}x_q(u))_q\) and \(y=(t^{s_a}y_a(u))_a\), define
   109	
   110	\[
   111	\begin{aligned}
   112	 \nu_X(x)&=\min_q\{r_q+6\operatorname{ord}_u x_q\},\\
   113	 \nu_Y(y)&=\min_a\{s_a+6\operatorname{ord}_u y_a\},
   114	\end{aligned}                                             \tag{1.5}
   115	\]
   116	
   117	with \(\nu(0)=\infty\). Then \(F^nX=\{x:\nu_X(x)\ge n\}\), and
   118	similarly for \(Y^+\). These filtrations are decreasing, separated, and
   119	complete, with finite-dimensional graded pieces.
   120	
   121	At a fully specified allowed source point \(s\), use the actual all-depth
   122	normalized orbit products in the map
   123	
   124	\[
   125	 \mathcal E(x)=(\theta\Phi-12\Phi)\Gamma_\eta
   126	       -\Phi_\eta(\theta\Gamma-18\Gamma)+42t^{20}.        \tag{1.6}
   127	\]
   128	
   129	Equation (1.6) uses the chosen gauge \(c_fc_g=1\); without it the last scalar
   130	coefficient is \(42/(c_fc_g)\). The B-orbits, finite \(u_f/v_f\) data, pole
