    12	
    13	## 1. Setup
    14	
    15	`A` = the CORE2 coordinate ring localized at the pole scales
    16	(`k[CORE2][1/W1W2]`, 27-variable unsplit / 22-variable fiber chart)
    17	— a finitely generated `k`-algebra, hence **Noetherian**. For each
    18	odd depth `D >= 21`, `V_D` = the residue-A window at depth `D`
    19	(B-frozen, no-log, `W != 0`: algebraic conditions of bounded
    20	degree), `pi_A : V_D -> Spec A` the projection killing the depth-D
    21	tails, `J_D = ker(A -> k[V_D])` the elimination (image) ideal.
    22	
    23	**Truncation-compatibility (machine fact, not hypothesis).** The
    24	depth-23 system CONTAINS the depth-21 system on the common
    25	variables: the D23-core's first 38 post-header rows are
    26	byte-identical to the banked D21 CORE2 rows at all three primes
    27	(gate DS1, live re-verification; the build-tier row-for-row
    28	regression is SHEET6-DIRECTIONB §8's 17/18 gate). New depths add
    29	rows (22 pivots + 10 Row_22 at D23), never remove them.
    30	
    31	## 2. Theorem DS (depth decision, residue-A window tier)
    32	
    33	**(1) Chain (route A, proved at this tier).** Truncation-
    34	compatibility gives `pi_A(V_23) ⊆ pi_A(V_21)`, hence
    35	`J_21 ⊆ J_23 ⊆ J_25 ⊆ ...` — an ascending chain in the Noetherian
    36	ring `A`. It stabilizes at some finite `D*`; equivalently the
    37	closed images `X_D = closure(pi_A(V_D))` descend and stabilize.
    38	Mod `p` the windows are finite sets, so a descending chain of
    39	nonempty images has nonempty inverse limit (König): **all depths
    40	nonempty ⟺ a mod-p formal germ exists.** The mechanism is
    41	exact-verified on a toy tower (gate DS2: `J_1 = (0) ⊊ J_2 ∋ b⁴−a³`).
    42	
    43	**(2) Kill direction (depth-free; what the running lanes consume).**
    44	A formal germ truncates to a point of every `V_D`; contrapositive:
    45	`V_D = ∅` at ANY single depth kills the germ (mod-p screening
    46	tier). **The pre-registered "D23 EMPTY at 2+ primes ⇒ residue-A
    47	dead at the window tier" needs no stabilization theorem — only
    48	DS1's compatibility.** (Gate DS3.)
    49	
    50	**(3) Live direction (route B, COMMITTED; the effective decision
    51	constant).** Tougeron's implicit function theorem (Greenberg's
    52	effective form for our data): *a depth-`D` point `s` whose Jacobian
    53	minor has t-adic valuation `e(s) <= (D−1)/2` lifts to a true formal
    54	germ agreeing with `s` to depth `D − e(s)`.* Decision constant:
    55	`D*_eff = 2e* + 1`, `e*` = the minimal minor-valuation over the
    56	depth-`D` locus. So if a depth-`D` lane returns NONEMPTY and a
    57	found point has `e <= (D−1)/2`, the LIVE verdict is **final** — the
    58	formal germ exists and the iteration stops. Newton mechanism
    59	demonstrated exactly mod 105337 (gate DS4a/b: `e = 1` point lifted
    60	from depth `3 = 2e+1` to depth 24); the hypothesis is load-bearing
    61	(DS4c: `x² = t³` is solvable mod `t³` with `e = ∞` and truly
    62	unsolvable — no lift is claimed there).
    63	
    64	**(4) Combined decision rule.** At the current depth `D`:
    65	`EMPTY (2+ primes)` ⇒ dead, final, by (2). `NONEMPTY` ⇒ compute
    66	`e` on the found points (recipe §3): if `e <= (D−1)/2` ⇒ LIVE,
    67	final, by (3) — at `D = 23` this means **any found point with
    68	`e <= 11` already decides the formal germ question**; else iterate
    69	to `D = 2e + 1` with the stopping rule now e-tracked, not
    70	open-ended. Either way **the depth-`D*` window verdict decides the
    71	formal germ question** with `D* = max(23, 2e* + 1)` on the live
    72	branch and `D* =` the first empty depth on the dead branch.
    73	
    74	## 3. The e-recipe (banked, gate DS6)
    75	
    76	For a found depth-`D` point `s` of the window system `F_1..F_m`
    77	(87 vars / 77 eqs at D23): `e(s) = min` over maximal minors `M` of
    78	`Jac(F)(s)` of the t-adic valuation of `M`. Cheap per point (rank
    79	computations over `F_p[t]/t^D`). Controls: a unit minor gives
    80	`e = 0`, `D* = 1` (`γ ≡ id` — the ctl0/origin window must behave
