    54	There is also an orientation error in the proposed $\eta^0$ membership test.
    55	Let
    56	
    57	\[
    58	 e_0=\operatorname{Row}_{20}[\eta^0]+42.
    59	\]
    60	
    61	Then $e_0$ is itself a generator of the J-window ideal.  Thus
    62	$e_0\in I_J$ with the one-term certificate $e_0=1\cdot e_0$; the exact
    63	unit-pivot compression does not change that fact after pullback/localization.
    64	This membership says nothing new.
    65	
    66	For set-theoretic novelty the correct tests are:
    67	
    68	- whether $e_0\in\sqrt{I_{Q2}}$ on a Q2 chart, if asking whether the J row
    69	  cuts that Q2 chart; or
    70	- whether a Q2 generator belongs to $\sqrt{I_J}$, if asking whether Q2 cuts
    71	  the J-window.
    72	
    73	Ordinary nonmembership alone is insufficient: $x\notin(x^2)$, although
    74	adding $(x)$ does not change the zero set.  Section 4 avoids this distinction
    75	entirely by proving that the relevant saturated ordinary ideal is already
    76	the unit ideal.
    77	
    78	## 2. Bounded algebraicity is invisible at D23
    79	
    80	Put $R=K[[T]]$, with $v(T)=1$, over a characteristic-zero field containing
    81	the template radicals and a primitive 42nd root of unity.  The following
    82	elementary form of clustered Hensel lifting is the main tool.
    83	
    84	### Lemma 2.1 (clustered truncation/Hensel lemma)
    85	
    86	Let $a_1,\ldots,a_n\in TR$ be pairwise distinct and
    87	
    88	\[
    89	 H(T,Y)=\prod_{j=1}^n(Y-a_j).
    90	\]
    91	
    92	For a fixed $i$, set
    93	
    94	\[
    95	 \lambda_{ij}=v(a_i-a_j),\qquad
    96	 d_i=\sum_{j\ne i}\lambda_{ij},\qquad
    97	 L_i=\max_{j\ne i}\lambda_{ij}.
    98	\]
    99	
   100	Suppose that $P\in R[Y]$ is monic of degree $n$ and
   101	$P-H\in T^eR[Y]$.  If $e>d_i+L_i$, then $P$ has a unique root
   102	$b_i\in a_i+T^{e-d_i}R$.  The roots obtained for different $i$ are
   103	distinct.  If a finite group fixes $P$ and permutes the $a_i$, it
   104	permutes the $b_i$ with the same labels.
   105	
   106	#### Proof
   107	
   108	Write
   109	
   110	\[
