   210	\(r_1\) representing such a root.
   211	
   212	It remains only to lift the congruence from \(p\) to \(p^3\). Suppose
   213	\(r_m^2-cs=p^md_m\) for \(m=1\) or \(2\). The class of \(2r_m\) is a
   214	unit modulo \(p\), so choose \(u_m\) with
   215	
   216	\[
   217	 d_m+2r_mu_m\equiv0\pmod p.
   218	\]
   219	
   220	For \(r_{m+1}=r_m+p^mu_m\), expansion gives
   221	
   222	\[
   223	 r_{m+1}^2-cs
   224	 =p^m\bigl(d_m+2r_mu_m+p^mu_m^2\bigr),
   225	\]
   226	
   227	which is divisible by \(p^{m+1}\). Two steps give \(r=r_3\) with
   228	\(p^3\mid r^2-cs\); defining \(Q=(r^2-cs)/p^3\) proves (6). \(\square\)
   229	
   230	Identity (5) is the special rigidity failure. It is the inhomogeneous
   231	equation
   232	
   233	\[
   234	 pC'-\frac23p'C=c,
   235	 \qquad C=\frac92cs. \tag{7}
   236	\]
   237	
   238	Here \(\deg C=4=(2/3)\deg p\), so the leading term is resonant. The
   239	promoted Theorem A applies to the integer weights \(w\ge1\) and uses the
   240	factor \(\deg C-w\deg A\) at infinity
   241	([MATHIEU.md:279-315](../MATHIEU.md)); it cannot be extended naively to
   242	the fractional weight \(2/3\), for which (7) is an explicit counterexample.
   243	
   244	## 4. Proof of Theorem B-O
   245	
   246	Choose \(r,Q\) as in Lemma 2 and define
   247	
   248	\[
   249	 a=\frac{pQ}{12},
   250	 \qquad
   251	 b=\frac32\frac{a^2}{p}=\frac{pQ^2}{96}.
   252	 \tag{8}
   253	\]
   254	
   255	Both are polynomials. Set
   256	
   257	\[
   258	\begin{aligned}
   259	 \Phi&=p^2+r t^{10}+a t^{20},\\
   260	 \Gamma&=p^3+\frac32pr t^{10}+6pa t^{20}+b t^{40}.
   261	\end{aligned}
   262	\tag{9}
   263	\]
   264	
   265	Only row indices \(0,10,20,30,40\) can occur below \(42\).
   266	The lead row \(R_0\) vanishes because \(p^2,p^3\) are a square/cube pair,
   267	and Lemma 1 gives \(R_{10}=0\).
   268	
   269	For Row \(20\), put
   270	
   271	\[
   272	 h_{20}:=G_{20}-\frac32pF_{20}
   273	 =\frac92pa
   274	 =\frac38\frac{r^2-cs}{p}.
   275	\]
   276	
   277	Combining (3) with the quadratic term (4) gives
   278	
   279	\[
   280	 R_{20}=9prr'-3p'r^2-12p^2h_{20}'-4pp'h_{20}.
   281	\]
   282	
   283	Defining
   284	
   285	\[
   286	 C:=\frac92r^2-12ph_{20}=\frac92cs,
   287	\]
   288	
   289	gives
   290	
   291	\[
   292	 R_{20}=pC'-\frac23p'C
   293	 =c\left(\frac92ps'-3p's\right)=c
   294	\]
   295	
   296	by (5).
   297	
   298	At Row \(30\), the only pairs are \((10,20)\) and \((20,10)\), hence
   299	
   300	\[
   301	\begin{aligned}
   302	 R_{30}
   303	 &=-2\bigl(rG_{20}\bigr)'
   304	   +8\bigl(aG_{10}\bigr)'\\
   305	 &=-2(6par)'+8\left(\frac32par\right)'=0.
   306	\end{aligned}
   307	\]
   308	
   309	At Row \(40\), the self-pair of the slot-20 coefficients contributes
   310	
   311	\[
   312	 8a(6pa)'-2(6pa)a'=48p'a^2+36paa'.
   313	\]
   314	
   315	The term linear in \(G_{40}=b\) is
   316	
   317	\[
   318	 -12p^2b'-44pp'b=-48p'a^2-36paa',
   319	\]
   320	
   321	where (8) was used. Thus \(R_{40}=0\). Every other row below \(42\)
   322	vanishes by support, proving (2). In fact the exact remainder is
   323	
   324	\[
   325	 \mathcal B(\Phi,\Gamma)
   326	 =ct^{20}+(-2rb'-22br')t^{50}
   327	          +(8ab'-22ba')t^{60}.
   328	 \tag{10}
   329	\]
   330	
   331	This completes the proof. \(\square\)
   332	
   333	## 5. What the associated graded does prove
   334	
   335	### Proposition 3 (no positive-valuation deformation of the skeleton)
   336	
   337	Let \((R,\mathfrak m)\) be a local ring with characteristic-zero residue
   338	field. Suppose a residue-A template jet through slot \(20\) has
   339	\(c_f,c_g\in R^\times\), and every free P-side and B-side tail coefficient
   340	lies in \(\mathfrak m\); the seven dead-stretch coefficients may be
   341	arbitrary elements of \(R\). Then the jet cannot satisfy the J-identity
   342	through slot \(20\).
   343	
   344	#### Proof
   345	
   346	Reduce every row modulo \(\mathfrak m\). Every tail-loaded monomial
   347	disappears, while \(-42/(c_fc_g)\) remains nonzero because the residue
   348	field has characteristic zero. The reduced equations are precisely the
   349	pure-dead-stretch zero-tail system: the full-window gate verifies that
   350	tail specialization reproduces that system coefficient-for-coefficient
   351	([SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md:389-396](../SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md)). The promoted
   352	zero-tail theorem rules it out uniformly in all seven dead-stretch
   353	coefficients ([SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md:221-238](../SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md)).
   354	Contradiction. \(\square\)
   355	
   356	Thus every possible survivor has at least one order-zero tail under any
   357	such valuation. The associated graded skeleton excludes infinitesimal or
   358	positive-valuation deformations, but it says nothing about the order-one
   359	quadratic escape constructed in (9). This agrees with the campaign's
   360	inconsistent differentials at the tested zero-tail points
   361	([SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md:503-518](../SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md)).
   362	
   363	## 6. Why a fixed additive moment cannot repair the argument
   364	
   365	The preceding theorem concerns unrestricted polynomial jets. There is
   366	also a narrower obstruction inside the campaign's actual 83-variable
   367	window.
   368	
   369	Fix a value-tier specialization for which the promoted free-monomial
   370	relaxation is reported consistent (in particular, the normalized verdict
   371	specialization), and base-change its étale coefficient algebra to an
   372	infinite field factor \(K\). Collect the 77 recorded eta-component
   373	equations into
   374	
   375	\[
   376	 r+P(u)=0,
   377	 \qquad
   378	 P(u)=\sum_{\alpha\in A}v_\alpha u^\alpha\in W:=K^{77},
   379	 \tag{11}
   380	\]
   381	
   382	where \(u\) is the tail/dead-stretch tuple, \(A\) is the finite set of
   383	distinct nonconstant monomials, and \(r\) is the zero-tail residual,
   384	including the pole-scale block and the Row-20 constant. The campaign's
   385	value-tier algebra and unit-pivot semantics are documented at
   386	[SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md:404-425](../SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md).
   387	
   388	### Lemma 4 (evaluation-span lemma)
   389	
   390	For any polynomial map of the form (11) over an infinite field,
   391	
   392	\[
   393	 \operatorname{span}_K\{P(u):u\in K^N\}
   394	 =\operatorname{span}_K\{v_\alpha:\alpha\in A\}. \tag{12}
   395	\]
   396	
   397	#### Proof
   398	
   399	The left side is contained in the right side by expansion. Let \(U\) be
   400	the left side and let \(\pi:W\to W/U\) be the quotient map. For every
   401	\(u\in K^N\),
   402	
   403	\[
   404	 \sum_{\alpha\in A}\pi(v_\alpha)u^\alpha=\pi(P(u))=0.
   405	\]
   406	
   407	Apply any linear functional \(\lambda\in(W/U)^*\). The resulting scalar
   408	polynomial vanishes on all of \(K^N\). Since \(K\) is infinite and the
   409	monomials are distinct, every coefficient
   410	\(\lambda(\pi(v_\alpha))\) is zero. Linear functionals separate points in
   411	the finite-dimensional space \(W/U\), so \(\pi(v_\alpha)=0\) for every
   412	\(\alpha\). Hence every \(v_\alpha\in U\), proving the reverse inclusion.
   413	\(\square\)
   414	
   415	The exact free-monomial computation reports:
   416	
   417	- Row \(20\): 10 rows, 2718 monomial columns, rank 10, consistent;
   418	- full window: 77 rows, 5106 monomial columns, rank 57, consistent.
   419	
   420	These are promoted campaign results
   421	([SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md:475-501](../SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md)). For Row \(20\),
   422	rank 10 says its coefficient vectors span the entire ten-dimensional
   423	eta-slot space. Lemma 4 therefore implies that the span of evaluated
   424	Row-20 tail contributions contains, in particular, the pure
   425	\(\eta^0\)-constant vector.
   426	
   427	Separately, consistency of the full affine system says
   428	\(-r\in\operatorname{span}\{v_\alpha\}\), and Lemma 4 upgrades this to
   429	
   430	\[
   431	 -r\in\operatorname{span}_K\{P(u):u\in K^{83}\}. \tag{13}
   432	\]
   433	
   434	Therefore, if a fixed linear functional annihilates the Row-20 tail
   435	contribution for every tail assignment, it annihilates the pure constant
   436	vector; and if \(\Lambda:W\to K\) annihilates the full contribution
   437	\(P(u)\) for every assignment, it also annihilates \(r\). Likewise, every
   438	fixed linear subspace containing all evaluated tail contributions contains
   439	their span, hence the corresponding constant vector and full residual.
   440	In particular, a residue, constant-term, Gaussian-moment functional, or
   441	proposed Mathieu subspace used **only in this unconditional
   442	additive-separation form** cannot contain all tails while excluding the
   443	nonzero constant. Here only the underlying linear subspace of a Mathieu
   444	subspace is being used
   445	([MATHIEU.md:37-47](../MATHIEU.md)); the conclusion does not exclude a
   446	power/multiplicative Mathieu argument after imposing the lower-band ideal.
   447	
   448	This does **not** produce one tail tuple solving the equations: (13) may
   449	use a linear combination of values at different tuples. Equivalently, the
   450	relaxation treats the monomial coordinates \(u^\alpha\) independently,
   451	whereas a real tuple must lie on their monomial (toric/Veronese) image.
   452	A functional formed only after restricting to the nonlinear lower-band
   453	variety is not excluded by this theorem; it would be using precisely the
   454	missing multiplicative information.
   455	
