   200	## 2. Delayed surjectivity and a causal right section
   201	
   202	The Round-6 delayed loss is the correct linear invariant:
   203	
   204	\[
   205	 \ell(L)=\min\left\{e\ge0:
   206	 F^{n+e}Y\subseteq L(F^nX)\ \text{for every }n\ge0\right\}. \tag{2.1}
   207	\]
   208	
   209	If the displayed set is empty, define \(\ell(L)=\infty\).
   210	
   211	It is useful to make explicit what (2.1) supplies.
   212	
   213	### Lemma 2.1 (delayed inclusion is equivalent to a bounded section)
   214	
   215	Let \(X,Y\) be finite sums of shifted one-variable formal-series spaces with
   216	the global-order filtration, and let \(L:X\to Y\) be continuous and
   217	\(k\)-linear. For an integer \(e\ge0\), the following are equivalent.
   218	
   219	1. \(F^{n+e}Y\subseteq L(F^nX)\) for every \(n\ge0\).
   220	2. There is a continuous \(k\)-linear map
   221	
   222	   \[
   223	   S:F^eY\longrightarrow X                              \tag{2.2}
   224	   \]
   225	
   226	   such that
   227	
   228	   \[
   229	   LS=\operatorname{id}_{F^eY},\qquad
   230	   S(F^{n+e}Y)\subseteq F^nX\quad(n\ge0).                \tag{2.3}
   231	   \]
   232	
   233	#### Proof
   234	
   235	The implication \(2\Rightarrow1\) is immediate. Conversely, split \(Y\) by
   236	exact global \(t\)-degree:
   237	
   238	\[
   239	 F^eY=\prod_{m\ge e}Y[m],
   240	\]
   241	
   242	where every \(Y[m]\) is finite-dimensional and is zero when degree \(m\) is
   243	not one of the allowed congruence classes. For a basis vector
   244	\(v\in Y[m]\), hypothesis 1 with \(n=m-e\) gives an
   245	\(x_v\in F^{m-e}X\) satisfying \(Lx_v=v\). Choose these preimages basis by
   246	basis and extend to a linear map \(S_m:Y[m]\to F^{m-e}X\).
   247	
   248	For \(y=\sum_{m\ge e}y_m\), set
   249	
   250	\[
   251	 Sy=\sum_{m\ge e}S_my_m.                                \tag{2.4}
   252	\]
   253	
   254	The sum converges because its \(m\)-th term lies in \(F^{m-e}X\).
   255	Continuity of \(L\) gives \(LSy=\sum_my_m=y\), and (2.3) follows degree by
   256	degree. \(\square\)
   257	
   258	Thus an all-depth proof of (2.1) is mathematically enough. For a machine
   259	certificate, however, the choices in (2.4) must be replaced by an exact Ore
   260	identity or a finite-state causal recurrence whose initial transient and
   261	all-index identities prove (2.3) for every degree; any claimed periodic part
   262	must itself be proved, including its boundary identities. A finite rank
   263	profile does not prove (2.1).
   264	
   265	We call any \(e\) for which (2.2)--(2.3) has been proved a **certified inverse
   266	loss**. It need not be minimal.
   267	
   268	### Remark 2.2 (relation to the ordinary DEPTH-STAB minor)
   269	
   270	For an \(R\)-linear square matrix \(A\) with
   271	\(\det A=t^e\varepsilon\), \(\varepsilon\in R^\times\), the adjugate formula
   272	
   273	\[
   274	 S=(\det A)^{-1}\operatorname{adj}(A):t^eR^r\longrightarrow R^r
   275	                                                                  \tag{2.5}
   276	\]
   277	
   278	is a causal right section of loss at most \(e\). Corollary 3.2 therefore
   279	recovers the usual \(2e+1\) minor criterion. In the present problem,
   280	\(L_s^+\) is only \(k\)-linear: \(\Theta u=u(\Theta+1)\), so an \(R\)-matrix
   281	adjugate does not apply. Equations (2.2)--(2.3) are the exact replacement,
   282	not an analogy based only on an Ore index.
   289	### Theorem 3.1 (filtered fixed-linearization Newton theorem)
   290	
   291	Let \(X,Y\) be complete separated filtered \(k\)-vector spaces, with linear
   292	topologies defined by \(F^nX,F^nY\). Let \(\mathcal H\) be a continuous map on
   293	the affine space \(s+F^1X\), and put
   294	
   295	\[
   296	 A=\mathcal H(s),\qquad L=D\mathcal H_s,\qquad
   297	 N(h)=\mathcal H(s+h)-A-Lh.                              \tag{3.1}
   298	\]
   299	
   300	Assume:
   301	
   302	1. \(A\in F^DY\);
   303	2. \(L\) has a causal right section \(S:F^eY\to X\) satisfying
   304	   (2.3); and
   305	3. for some fixed integer \(\sigma\ge0\), the nonlinear remainder satisfies
   306	
   307	   \[
   308	   N(F^aX)\subseteq F^{2a-\sigma}Y                       \tag{3.2}
   309	   \]
   310	
   311	   and, whenever \(h,h'\in F^aX\) and
   312	   \(h-h'\in F^mX\), \(m\ge a\),
   313	
   314	   \[
   315	   N(h)-N(h')\in F^{a+m-\sigma}Y.                        \tag{3.3}
   316	   \]
   317	
   318	If
   319	
   320	\[
   321	                       D\ge2e+\sigma+1,                  \tag{3.4}
   322	\]
   323	
   324	then there is an \(h_\infty\in F^{D-e}X\) such that
   325	
   326	\[
   327	                       \mathcal H(s+h_\infty)=0.         \tag{3.5}
   328	\]
   329	
   330	Only the fixed derivative \(D\mathcal H_s\) is used.
   331	
   332	#### Proof
   333	
   334	Put
   335	
   336	\[
   337	 q=D-e,
   338	\]
   339	
   340	so (3.4) gives \(q-e-\sigma=D-2e-\sigma\ge1\). On the
   341	complete ball \(B=F^qX\), define
   342	
   343	\[
   344	 T(h)=-S\bigl(A+N(h)\bigr).                              \tag{3.6}
   345	\]
   346	
   347	This is defined because \(A\in F^DY=F^{q+e}Y\subseteq F^eY\), while
   348	
   349	\[
   350	 N(h)\in F^{2q-\sigma}Y\subseteq F^{q+e}Y                \tag{3.7}
   351	\]
   352	
   353	by \(q\ge e+\sigma\). The section bound now gives \(T(h)\in F^qX\), so
   354	\(T\) maps \(B\) to itself.
   355	
   356	If \(h,h'\in B\) and \(h-h'\in F^mX\), \(m\ge q\), then (3.3) and
   357	(2.3) give
   358	
   359	\[
   360	\begin{aligned}
   361	 T(h)-T(h')
   362	 &=-S\bigl(N(h)-N(h')\bigr)\\
   363	 &\in F^{q+m-\sigma-e}X\\
   364	 &=F^{m+c}X,
   365	 \qquad c:=D-2e-\sigma\ge1.                             \tag{3.8}
   366	\end{aligned}
   367	\]
   368	
   369	Start with \(h_0=0\) and set \(h_{j+1}=T(h_j)\). Both \(h_0,h_1\) lie in
   370	\(F^qX\), and (3.8) inductively yields
   371	
   372	\[
   373	 h_{j+1}-h_j\in F^{q+jc}X.                              \tag{3.9}
   374	\]
   375	
   376	Hence \((h_j)\) is Cauchy and converges, by completeness, to some
   377	\(h_\infty\in F^qX\). The same estimates make \(T\) continuous on \(B\),
   378	so \(h_\infty=T(h_\infty)\). Applying \(L\) and using \(LS=1\) gives
   379	
   380	\[
   381	 Lh_\infty=-A-N(h_\infty).
   382	\]
   383	
   384	Together with (3.1), this is (3.5). \(\square\)
   385	
   386	### Corollary 3.2 (zero quadratic loss)
   387	
   388	When \(\sigma=0\), the exact integer-depth gate is
   389	
   390	\[
   391	                  \boxed{D\ge2e+1}
   392	       \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
   393	                  \boxed{e\le\left\lfloor\frac{D-1}{2}\right\rfloor}. \tag{3.10}
   394	\]
   395	
   396	More sharply, if the actual residual order is
   397	\(\nu=\nu_Y(\mathcal H(s))\), it is enough that
   398	
   399	\[
   400	                            \boxed{\nu>2e}.               \tag{3.11}
   401	\]
   402	
   403	The correction has order at least \(\nu-e\). In particular, this theorem
   404	does **not** promise a formal root with the entire depth-\(D\) jet fixed. To
   405	obtain congruence modulo \(F^A\), and hence preserve all coefficients of order
   406	strictly below \(A\), one needs \(\nu\ge A+e\), in addition to (3.11). To
   407	preserve the coefficient of \(t^A\) as well, one needs
   408	\(\nu\ge A+e+1\). If \(\nu=\infty\), the starting point is already a root
   409	and no correction is needed.
   410	
   500	same proof applies. Being a unit at each point, without both the filtered
   501	difference estimates and the quadratic audit of \(U\), is not enough. What
   502	is also not allowed without accounting for it is an unrecorded negative power
   503	of \(t\); a normalization losing \(\sigma\) orders must use Theorem 3.1 with
   504	that \(\sigma\).
   505	
   506	The full expression (1.6) is built from products, \(\eta\)-differentiation,
   507	and \(\theta\). Therefore, once `CYCLIC-30` identifies the all-depth streams
   508	and `FILTER-30` exhibits the source-dependent normalized products on the
   509	whole Newton ball as integral filtered expressions, Lemma 4.2 proves the
   510	required quadratic estimate with \(\sigma=0\). The Euler terms themselves
   511	cause no additional Hensel loss; \(\eta\)-differentiation changes only the
   512	finite component label and does not change \(t\)-order.
   513	
   514	Characteristic-\(p\) resonance is instead a **linear inverse** issue. For
   515	example, on \(k[[u]]\) in characteristic \(p\),
   516	
   517	\[
   518	 (\Theta+1)u^j=(j+1)u^j.                                 \tag{4.5}
   519	\]
   520	
   521	Every truncation ending before \(u^{p-1}\) is invertible, but the operator
   522	misses \(u^{p-1},u^{2p-1},\ldots\). Its all-depth delayed loss is infinite.
   523	This is a direct counterexample to promoting a low-window rank profile or an
   524	apparently stable defect without closing the full \(p\)-periodic recurrence.
